"""Non-graphic recognition, prevention and specialist safeguarding boundaries."""
def course(c,m):
 return c("SWP-054","Child Marriage FGM and Harmful Practices","child-marriage-fgm-and-harmful-practices","Child Marriage FGM\nand Harmful Practices",
 "Analyse non-graphic fictional harmful-practice concerns through rights, best interests and qualified safeguarding, distinguishing prevention evidence from clinical or legal competence.",[
m("Rights context and respectful recognition","Recognise harmful-practice concerns without stereotyping, investigation or unsupported legal conclusions.",[
"Child marriage and female genital mutilation raise distinct protection, health and rights concerns. They should not be collapsed into one experience or treated as an inevitable characteristic of any community. Discuss the reported concern, the person's access to support and the appropriate qualified role rather than infer practices from ethnicity, religion, nationality or appearance. A respectful response can challenge harm while recognising the person as a rights-holder with priorities and strengths. This course uses non-graphic fictional scenarios and does not ask learners to identify affected classmates, inspect bodies or investigate families. [S1, S10, S14]",
"Social and economic pressures may shape the choices available to girls and families, but a contextual explanation does not excuse harm. Community leaders' views are not substitutes for an individual child's welfare or meaningful participation. The updated child-marriage chapter addresses girls at risk and girls who are already married, separated, divorced or widowed. Support must not disappear because a prevention opportunity has passed. Educational, psychosocial, protection and qualified health needs can remain relevant, with a coordinated response appropriate to the person's circumstances rather than a single label or public campaign category. [S11, S10]",
"Recognition is different from establishing facts, making a diagnosis or determining legal status. An introductory learner should not decide whether a particular marriage is valid, issue a universal statement about Nigerian reporting duties or infer an imminent threat from a demographic characteristic. Applicable jurisdiction, organisational safeguarding procedures and qualified advice matter. If an actual concern arises, follow authorised safeguarding and emergency arrangements appropriate to the setting rather than use a course exercise as a decision protocol. Avoid promising absolute secrecy, confronting a suspected actor or arranging informal family mediation. [S11, S1]",
"The 2025 WHO overview expands guidance on FGM prevention and care, including education and health-worker capacity. Reading its overview does not qualify a learner to examine, classify or treat FGM. Qualified care should be accessible without stigma, and involvement by a health worker does not make a harmful practice acceptable. A fictional support note can identify a need for appropriate clinical expertise while excluding anatomical details, procedural advice and medical claims. Keep the person's dignity, privacy and practical access needs visible; never make coursework depend on graphic description or personal testimony. [S14, S10, S1]"],
"Fictional case: a youth service hears a non-graphic concern about a girl leaving school for marriage. A learner recognises a safeguarding concern and the need for qualified advice, without deciding legal status, contacting the family or naming the girl in class.",
"Write a recognition note stating the concern, two rights/access questions, an uncertainty, the appropriate specialist role and three actions outside this learner's scope. Use no identifying or graphic details.",
"A sound note challenges harm without stereotyping. It distinguishes concern from proof, preserves dignity and routes child protection, legal and health questions through qualified procedures rather than personal investigation.",["S1","S10","S11","S14"],[
("Harmful practices should not be inferred from group identity.","A learner assumes a family practises FGM solely from its community identity. What is the appropriate correction?","Address actual concerns and support needs without identity-based assumptions.",["Treat the stereotype as confirmed evidence.","Ask classmates to identify affected families.","Use appearance to determine an individual's experience."],"Group identity cannot establish an individual's experience or a practice."),
("Support remains relevant after a marriage has occurred.","A fictional girl is already married and needs education and protection support. What follows from the updated guidance?","Her current safety, wishes and support needs still require appropriate specialist attention.",["Prevention having passed makes all support irrelevant.","Only unmarried girls may receive assistance.","A marital label replaces assessment of her circumstances."],"The updated chapter includes girls in several marital situations."),
("Recognition is separate from a legal determination.","A course participant is asked to decide whether a fictional Nigerian marriage is legally valid. What fits the course boundary?","Identify the need for jurisdiction-specific qualified legal and safeguarding advice.",["Issue a universal legal ruling from the course title.","Assume all reporting duties are identical everywhere.","Treat a classroom answer as a court decision."],"The course does not establish local legal status or reporting rules."),
("A WHO overview does not confer clinical competence.","After reading the FGM guideline overview, what can an introductory learner appropriately do?","Recognise the need for non-stigmatising access to qualified care within role boundaries.",["Perform an examination for a course assessment.","Give procedural treatment instructions.","Diagnose FGM from a second-hand description."],"The comparison concerns prevention and qualified access, not clinical training."),
("Health-worker involvement does not legitimise a harmful practice.","A fictional discussion suggests FGM becomes acceptable if performed by a health worker. What principle applies?","Medical involvement does not make the harmful practice acceptable.",["Professional clothing removes the rights concern.","Medicalisation is the course's prevention solution.","Any clinical setting guarantees that no harm occurs."],"Prevention and qualified care must not be confused with endorsing medicalisation."),
]),
m("Best interests assent and safe support","Identify child-specific communication and safeguarding needs without applying an adult consent shortcut.",[
"A child's best interests require careful attention to safety, wellbeing, rights and the likely effects of proposed actions. They are not simply an adult's preference or a convenient reason to ignore the child's views. The updated child-marriage chapter emphasises evolving capacities and age-appropriate communication. Qualified staff should help a child understand relevant options in ways suited to development, language and disability needs. In this introductory exercise, describe why a specialist process is needed and what uncertainties should be communicated; do not attempt a capacity assessment, a best-interests determination or a real case interview. [S11, S1]",
"Consent and assent are related but distinct. A child's affirmative participation is not automatically equivalent to an adult's legal authorisation, and a guardian's involvement is not automatically safe or sufficient for every action. Qualified procedures must consider applicable law, decision-specific capacity, safe caregiver involvement, the child's views and potential conflicts of interest. The course does not supply a universal age table or reporting rule. Do not assume the accompanying adult should receive a disclosure, or that a form signed by any adult resolves confidentiality and safeguarding concerns. [S11, S10]",
"A child may not use the language of abuse or may describe mixed feelings about a proposed marriage. Respectful listening does not require the child to adopt a label before accessing appropriate support. Avoid repeated questioning, promises about outcomes and pressure to provide proof. A learner should not contact a family, broker an agreement or direct the child to confront someone. Actual concerns need the designated qualified safeguarding process, with urgent needs handled through authorised arrangements. Explain foreseeable limits to privacy honestly through appropriate communication rather than promise that information can never be shared. [S11, S1]",
"A bounded handover identifies the concern and the responsible qualified role, using only necessary information through an approved channel. Do not place names, locations, incident narratives or images in course submissions. In a fictional plan, show how communication access, a safe contact route and follow-up responsibility would be considered by authorised staff. Where information is missing, retain uncertainty instead of resolving it by assumption. Specialist supervision matters because well-intended actions, including an unplanned family conversation or an unsafe contact attempt, may increase risk. The exercise assesses reasoning about boundaries, not readiness for independent child casework. [S11, S10]"],
"Fictional case: an adolescent asks a youth-service worker about continuing school and says the adult accompanying her should not hear the conversation. The learner identifies child-specific safeguarding and communication questions without assuming the adult is a safe guardian or promising an outcome.",
"Draft a supervised support outline: child participation, consent/assent distinction, caregiver uncertainty, privacy limits, a qualified decision route and safe follow-up responsibility. Explain why an adult-only consent approach is inadequate.",
"A defensible outline considers best interests and evolving capacities without replacing specialist decisions. It does not equate assent with legal consent, assume caregiver safety or initiate a family intervention.",["S11","S10","S1"],[
("Best interests include the child's views and potential effects of action.","An adult says a child's views are irrelevant because the adult knows best. What needs correction?","The qualified process should consider the child's views, rights, safety and wellbeing.",["Best interests always means the most convenient adult preference.","A child's participation has no place in safeguarding.","One adult statement settles every potential consequence."],"Best interests are not a shortcut for disregarding the child."),
("Assent is not automatically adult legal authorisation.","A fictional adolescent agrees to talk. What should a learner avoid assuming?","That this alone authorises every referral, disclosure or legal decision.",["That age-appropriate communication can be useful.","That qualified procedures remain necessary.","That the child's views should be heard respectfully."],"Participation does not remove decision-specific consent, legal and safeguarding requirements."),
("An accompanying adult is not necessarily a safe recipient.","A youth-service worker is uncertain whether an accompanying adult is safe to involve. What is appropriate?","Use the designated qualified safeguarding process rather than assume disclosure is safe.",["Automatically send the entire account to that adult.","Invite the adult to mediate without review.","Treat physical accompaniment as complete authorisation."],"Caregiver involvement needs a context-specific safety and authority assessment."),
("A child need not adopt an abuse label before receiving support.","A fictional girl discusses a proposed marriage without calling it violence. What response fits this course?","Respectful age-appropriate listening and the qualified support pathway.",["Withhold help until she uses a prescribed label.","Require a detailed proof narrative in class.","Ask learners to confront the family about her wording."],"Access to support should not depend on accepting a label."),
("A course exercise cannot authorise real child casework.","A learner completes the safeguarding worksheet successfully. What remains necessary for independent specialist work?","Appropriate professional role, qualified procedures, training and supervised competency assessment.",["Nothing beyond downloading the worksheet.","A public account of a real child's experience.","Permission inferred from a course completion badge."],"The exercise demonstrates introductory reasoning, not specialist authority or competence."),
]),
m("Prevention evidence and coordinated access","Appraise FGM education evidence and propose a bounded support/prevention review.",[
"Prevention of harmful practices needs respectful engagement, meaningful participation and links to appropriate services. A public promise or awareness activity may be a useful process observation, but it does not establish that harm has ceased. Avoid framing community participation as a request to reveal affected individuals or demonstrate compliance through inspections. A fictional prevention proposal can identify an educational aim, responsible facilitator, access needs and safeguarding dependencies. Clinical, legal and specialist child-protection decisions belong to qualified roles, and a prevention organiser should not make service use conditional on public agreement with the campaign. [S10, S1, S14]",
"The 2026 review of education and FGM prevention includes four observational studies searched through May 2023. Its findings are rated very low certainty and concern knowledge, attitudes, reported commitment and requests for reinfibulation. These outcomes differ from measured incidence of FGM. Neither the later publication year nor Nigerian authorship converts the findings into proof that a particular Nigerian counselling approach prevents FGM. Describe education as an evidence-informed option requiring contextual design and evaluation; do not promise an effect, reproduce a source intervention or present the review title as proof of every counselling format. [R26, S14]",
"Coordinated access means the relevant qualified services understand their roles and the person can consider suitable support within appropriate safeguarding arrangements. A health need, school-access problem and child-protection concern may require different expertise and information permissions. Do not send a full narrative to every provider because several services might be useful. Check actual service scope and authorised communication, and keep unresolved access barriers visible. A learner's fictional outline can specify responsibilities and review questions; it cannot promise care availability, direct a treatment, decide mandatory reporting or certify that a referral has been completed. [S11, S10, S1]",
"A limited review should separate what was delivered, what participants understood and what remains unknown about behaviour and harm. Examine who could not participate and whether discussion created stigma, pressure or identifying information. Any real assessment involving children or sensitive GBV information needs appropriate specialist ethical and safeguarding arrangements. In this course, use only the supplied fictional cases to identify a defensible next decision, an evidence gap and an accountable owner. Course completion, a campaign pledge and an approved professional service are different achievements; none should be substituted for another in a programme report. [R26, S10, S1]"],
"Fictional case: an organiser reports that an educational meeting ended FGM because participants expressed disapproval. A learner retains the observed learning result, removes the unsupported incidence claim and adds qualified safeguarding, access and evidence-review requirements.",
"Prepare a prevention-review note with the observed outcome, its evidence limit, a potential participation harm, qualified support roles and a review owner. Explain why stated commitment is not a measured incidence reduction.",
"A careful note uses the review's very low-certainty findings honestly. It supports respectful prevention and coordinated access without claiming clinical competence, legal authority or a demonstrated reduction in harm.",["R26","S14","S11","S10","S1"],[
("Knowledge and commitment outcomes differ from incidence.","Participants express opposition to FGM after a fictional lesson. Which conclusion fits the observation?","Reported views changed in that exercise; reduced incidence has not been demonstrated.",["FGM incidence necessarily fell by the same amount.","Every participant's future behaviour is known.","The observation establishes a clinical outcome."],"Attitude or commitment measures cannot substitute for incidence evidence."),
("The FGM education review reports very low-certainty evidence.","How should the four-study 2026 FGM review inform a proposed local educational activity?","As limited evidence requiring contextual appraisal and cautious claims.",["As a guaranteed effect for every counselling format.","As proof that all included studies were Nigerian trials.","As a reason to omit safeguarding and evaluation."],"The observational designs and certainty limits constrain transfer and claims."),
("Publication year does not identify the evidence search endpoint.","The FGM education review appeared in 2026 but searched to May 2023. What is accurate?","Its publication and search dates are distinct and should both inform appraisal.",["All evidence was gathered in January 2026.","The review necessarily includes every 2025 study.","A current issue date guarantees current local evidence."],"Research currency depends on the evidence period as well as publication."),
("Coordination does not permit unrestricted information sharing.","Several providers may assist a fictional child. What should the support outline specify?","Qualified roles, appropriate permissions and minimum necessary information for each purpose.",["Automatic distribution of the full account to every provider.","A shared classroom folder of case histories.","One campaign leader deciding all treatment and legal questions."],"Distinct needs and providers do not remove confidentiality and role boundaries."),
("A prevention review must consider unintended harms.","A campaign uses identifying community pledges to demonstrate success. What concern deserves review?","Pressure, stigma or identification may undermine safe participation.",["A public signature guarantees freedom from coercion.","Campaign visibility removes privacy duties.","Only the number of printed pledges matters."],"Participation quality and possible harms matter alongside delivery counts."),
])])
